Lab 18: Managing Token Keys
Objectives
Understand the importance of token key management in Keystone
Implement key rotation policies for Keystone token keys
Securely back up and synchronize token keys across controller nodes
Use OpenStack commands to manage token keys effectively
Prerequisites
OpenStack environment with Keystone installed (minimum Queens release)
Administrative access to the OpenStack controller nodes
Basic understanding of OpenStack authentication mechanisms
Familiarity with Linux command line and text editing (vim/nano)
Lab Setup
This lab requires:

At least one OpenStack controller node
Keystone service installed and configured
Root or sudo privileges on the controller node(s)
Task 1: Implement Key Rotation Policies
Subtask 1.1: Understand Keystone Token Key Configuration
Examine the current token provider configuration:
sudo grep -i token /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
Check the current token keys location:
sudo ls -l /var/lib/keystone/fernet-keys/
Expected Output:
You should see numbered key files (0, 1) and possibly more depending on your configuration.

Subtask 1.2: Configure Key Rotation Policy
Open the Keystone configuration file:
sudo vim /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
Locate or add the [fernet_tokens] section and configure:
[fernet_tokens]
key_repository = /var/lib/keystone/fernet-keys
max_active_keys = 3
rotation_frequency = 86400
Restart the Keystone service:
sudo systemctl restart apache2
Troubleshooting Tip:
If you encounter errors, check logs with:

sudo tail -f /var/log/keystone/keystone.log
Task 2: Securely Back Up and Synchronize Token Keys
Subtask 2.1: Create Key Backup
Create a secure backup directory:
sudo mkdir -p /secure-backup/keystone-keys
sudo chmod 700 /secure-backup/keystone-keys
Backup current keys:
sudo cp -a /var/lib/keystone/fernet-keys/* /secure-backup/keystone-keys/
Subtask 2.2: Synchronize Keys Across Controllers
On the primary controller, package the keys:
sudo tar czvf keystone-keys-backup.tar.gz /var/lib/keystone/fernet-keys/
Securely transfer to secondary controllers (replace with your actual IPs):
scp keystone-keys-backup.tar.gz root@controller2:/tmp/
On secondary controllers, restore the keys:
sudo tar xzvf /tmp/keystone-keys-backup.tar.gz -C /
sudo chown -R keystone:keystone /var/lib/keystone/fernet-keys
sudo systemctl restart apache2
Security Note:
Always use secure transfer methods (SCP/SFTP) and consider encrypting the backup.

Task 3: Manage Token Keys Using OpenStack Commands
Subtask 3.1: Rotate Keys Manually
Rotate the Fernet keys:
sudo keystone-manage fernet_rotate --keystone-user keystone --keystone-group keystone
Verify the rotation:
sudo ls -l /var/lib/keystone/fernet-keys/
Expected Outcome:
You should see a new key with the highest number (e.g., 2 if you previously had 0 and 1).

Subtask 3.2: Validate Token Functionality
Create a test token:
openstack token issue
Verify the token:
openstack token validate <token_id>
Troubleshooting Tip:
If token validation fails, ensure all controllers have synchronized keys and services are restarted.

Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Configured and implemented Keystone token key rotation policies
Established secure backup procedures for token keys
Synchronized keys across multiple controller nodes
Used OpenStack commands to manage and validate token keys
Proper token key management is crucial for maintaining a secure OpenStack environment. Regular key rotation and secure key distribution help prevent authentication issues and maintain system security.

